 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "in-plain-english",
   "heading": "In plain English",
   "role": "intuition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "in-plain-english-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "A Taylor approximation replaces a complicated curve, near a particular point, with something simple: first a straight line that matches the curve's height and slope there, then a parabola that also matches its curvature. Close to the point the approximation is excellent; far away it can be badly off. Much of economics quietly relies on the straight-line version — reading a log change as a percentage, or a regression line as a local slope."
      },
      {
       "t": "callout",
       "kind": "why",
       "title": "Why it matters",
       "md": "It tells you when everyday approximations are safe, and underlies second-order conditions and the delta method for standard errors.",
       "folded": false
      }
     ],
     "words": 95,
     "symbols": []
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "definition",
   "heading": "Definition",
   "role": "definition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "definition-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "One variable, around $a$:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "f(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\tfrac12 f''(a)(x-a)^2 + \\dots"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Many variables, around $\\mathbf{x}$, for a small step $\\mathbf{d}$:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "f(\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{d}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{x}) + \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x})'\\mathbf{d} + \\tfrac12\\,\\mathbf{d}'H(\\mathbf{x})\\,\\mathbf{d}"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "([Partial Derivatives and the Gradient](note:partial-derivatives-and-the-gradient), [Hessian Matrix](note:hessian-matrix))"
      }
     ],
     "words": 17,
     "symbols": [
      "$f$",
      "$a$, $\\mathbf{x}$",
      "$x$, $\\mathbf{d}$",
      "$\\nabla f$, $H$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "worked-example-the-log-approximation",
   "heading": "Worked example — the log approximation",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "worked-example-the-log-approximation-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "$\\ln(1+x) = x - \\tfrac{x^2}{2} + \\tfrac{x^3}{3} - \\dots$"
      },
      {
       "t": "table",
       "md": "| $x$ | $\\ln(1+x)$ | First order ($x$) | Second order ($x - x^2/2$) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0.05 | 0.0488 | 0.05 | 0.0488 |\n| 0.10 | 0.0953 | 0.10 | 0.0950 |\n| 0.25 | 0.2231 | 0.25 | 0.2188 |\n| 0.50 | 0.4055 | 0.50 | 0.3750 |"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "\"Log change ≈ percentage change\" is the first-order term, accurate only for small changes ([Logarithms and Growth Rates](note:logarithms-and-growth-rates))."
      }
     ],
     "words": 57,
     "symbols": [
      "$x$, $\\mathbf{d}$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "why-second-order-conditions-work",
   "heading": "Why second-order conditions work",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "why-second-order-conditions-work-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "At a critical point $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{0}$, so $f(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{d}) - f(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx \\tfrac12\\mathbf{d}'H\\mathbf{d}$. Its sign for every direction $\\mathbf{d}$ is the definiteness of $H$ ([Unconstrained Optimization](note:unconstrained-optimization))."
      }
     ],
     "words": 16,
     "symbols": [
      "$f$",
      "$a$, $\\mathbf{x}$",
      "$x$, $\\mathbf{d}$",
      "$\\nabla f$, $H$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "beyond-the-delta-method",
   "heading": "Beyond: the delta method",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "beyond-the-delta-method-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "The full treatment, with regression examples, is in [Delta Method](note:delta-method)."
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "For an estimator $\\hat\\theta$ with variance $V$, $\\mathrm{Var}(g(\\hat\\theta)) \\approx g'(\\theta)^2\\,V$ — a first-order Taylor expansion. It gives standard errors for transformed estimates such as an elasticity or $\\sigma = -1/\\beta$."
      }
     ],
     "words": 31,
     "symbols": [
      "$\\hat{\\theta}$, $\\theta$",
      "$V$",
